TCP delivery|TCP provides an ordered byte stream between endpoints, with retransmission and flow control.|Sequence numbers identify bytes and acknowledgments indicate progress. Lost segments are retransmitted; congestion control adjusts sending rate. A successful send does not prove the remote application committed a transaction.|ss -ti
UDP semantics|UDP sends independent datagrams without transport-level delivery or ordering guarantees.|Applications choose whether to add retries, ordering or congestion control. A missing reply may reflect loss, filtering or an application timeout. UDP's small transport overhead does not make reliability and capacity concerns disappear.|
DNS records|DNS stores typed records under hierarchical names, including A, AAAA, CNAME, MX and TXT.|Resolvers ask authoritative servers or caches for a specific record type. A CNAME points to another name rather than an IP address. Query the requested type and inspect the answer and authority sections before assuming a name is absent.|dig example.com A
DNS caching|DNS resolvers cache answers according to record TTLs and local policy.|A changed authoritative answer may coexist with older cached answers until their lifetime expires. Lower TTLs help future changes only after previously cached longer TTLs expire. Applications may also maintain their own caches.|dig example.com
Subnets|A subnet groups IP addresses sharing a network prefix and determines which destinations are considered on-link.|CIDR notation expresses prefix length; more prefix bits mean fewer addresses. Hosts use routes to decide whether to reach a neighbor directly or send packets through a gateway. Address overlap can make private-network connectivity ambiguous.|ip address show
Default routes|A default route is the fallback route used when no more-specific destination route matches.|Routing uses longest-prefix matching before metrics among equivalent candidates. The selected route determines next hop, interface and often source address. Check the actual route to the failing destination rather than only the configured gateway.|ip route get 192.0.2.10
ARP|ARP maps an on-link IPv4 address to a link-layer address such as an Ethernet MAC address.|A host broadcasts a request and caches the reply in its neighbor table. It resolves the gateway's MAC for off-link traffic, not the remote server's MAC. Failed or stale neighbor entries can prevent otherwise correct routing.|ip neigh show
Ports|A transport port identifies an application endpoint within a host's transport protocol.|TCP and UDP have separate port spaces. Connections are distinguished by protocol and endpoint addresses/ports; ephemeral source ports let clients create many connections. A bound listener, firewall allowance and application health are separate requirements.|ss -lntu
TCP handshake|The TCP handshake establishes connection state through SYN, SYN-ACK and ACK messages.|A repeated SYN without a reply suggests loss, filtering or unreachable service; a reset often indicates an explicit rejection. Capturing both ends distinguishes whether requests arrive and replies return. A completed handshake does not establish HTTP or TLS success.|
TLS identity|TLS encrypts a connection and can authenticate peers using certificates and a trust chain.|The client validates trust, validity dates and the requested hostname. SNI tells the server which name is requested, while hostname verification checks the certificate identity. Encryption alone is not enough if certificate verification is disabled.|openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com -verify_return_error -verify_hostname example.com
HTTP status|HTTP status codes classify a server's response to an application-layer request.|2xx indicates success at the HTTP layer, 4xx usually concerns the request or authorization, and 5xx indicates a server-side failure. Proxies and backends may generate different codes. Correlate response headers, body and request IDs to locate the source.|curl -I https://example.com
NAT state|Network address translation rewrites addresses or ports and often keeps connection-tracking state.|Return packets must match a live mapping. Idle expiration or exhausted translation ports can break connections without changing application code. Compare timeout policies and connection reuse across clients, gateways and load balancers.|
Firewall direction|Firewall rules constrain traffic based on fields such as source, destination, protocol and connection state.|Stateful filtering usually tracks return traffic; stateless rules must allow both directions explicitly. Routing and filtering are separate checks. Evaluate the path and actual source address after translation before broadening access.|
MTU|The maximum transmission unit limits packet size over a link.|Path MTU discovery learns a usable size across links, often through ICMP feedback. Blocking that feedback can allow small requests while larger transfers stall. Overlay headers reduce effective payload capacity; avoid assuming every path supports the same MTU.|ip link show
Packet loss|Packet loss occurs when packets are discarded in transit or at an endpoint.|Loss may arise from congestion, faulty links, policing or overloaded receivers. TCP retransmissions can mask loss while increasing latency. Intermediate devices may deprioritize diagnostic replies, so a single traceroute hop's loss is not proof of end-to-end loss.|
DNS negative caching|DNS caches can retain negative answers such as a name or record type not existing.|The authoritative response supplies information used to determine the negative caching lifetime. A newly created record can remain invisible to clients using a cached negative result. Compare authoritative and recursive answers before repeatedly changing the record.|
Keepalive|Keepalive mechanisms periodically test or preserve idle connections at a chosen protocol layer.|TCP keepalive, HTTP reuse and application heartbeats have different semantics. Their intervals must be considered alongside intermediaries' idle timeouts. A live socket does not prove the application is healthy or making progress.|ss -o state established
Connection pooling|A connection pool reuses established connections to reduce handshake cost and limit concurrency.|A bounded pool can protect a dependency, but requests may wait for a free connection. Stale idle connections, leaked leases and oversized pools cause different symptoms. Measure pool wait time separately from network and server execution time.|
Load balancing|A load balancer distributes requests or connections among backends according to policy and health.|Layer-4 balancing handles transport flows; layer-7 balancing can inspect HTTP routes and headers. Health checks need to reflect readiness without amplifying failures. Session affinity can improve locality but may cause uneven load.|
Proxy headers|Forwarding headers carry information about the original request across proxies.|Headers such as Forwarded or X-Forwarded-For can preserve client address and scheme. Clients can spoof them unless a trusted proxy removes or validates incoming values. Applications should trust only the configured proxy chain.|
Retries|A retry repeats an operation after a failure or uncertain outcome.|Bound retries by a deadline and use backoff with jitter to avoid synchronized overload. A timeout may occur after a server committed a write, so replay safety depends on idempotency or deduplication. Retrying at multiple layers can multiply traffic.|
Timeout budgets|A timeout budget limits how long an end-to-end operation may consume across its stages.|Connection, TLS, request, queue and dependency timeouts should fit within the caller's deadline. Cancel work when the deadline expires so abandoned requests do not keep consuming capacity. Longer timeouts can increase concurrent load during an outage.|
Asymmetric routing|Asymmetric routing means forward and return traffic take different paths.|This can be legitimate, but stateful firewalls or reverse-path filtering may reject traffic when they cannot associate both directions. Inspect routes and captures at both ends before changing filters. The client-side route alone describes only half the conversation.|
Anycast|Anycast advertises the same service address from multiple locations.|Routing policy selects a reachable path; it does not directly measure application health or geographic distance. Route changes can move clients to another location. Connection state and failure withdrawal behavior must be considered for stateful services.|
BGP policy|BGP exchanges reachability information between routing domains and selects paths according to policy.|Attributes and local policy influence selection rather than a simple shortest physical route. Prefix filters and route validation constrain accidental or unauthorized announcements. A valid advertised route does not guarantee a healthy application behind it.|
IPv6 neighbor discovery|IPv6 Neighbor Discovery uses ICMPv6 to find neighbors, routers and link information.|It replaces IPv4 ARP and supports functions including router discovery and duplicate address detection. Blocking all ICMPv6 can break normal connectivity and path MTU discovery. Link-local addressing is important for local routing communication.|ip -6 neigh show
TCP TIME_WAIT|TIME_WAIT retains state after active TCP close so delayed segments cannot be confused with a new connection.|Many entries can be normal with high connection churn. Investigate reuse, ephemeral-port availability and workload before changing kernel timeouts. Removing this protection indiscriminately can introduce subtle connection failures.|ss -tan state time-wait
Queueing|Queues buffer bursts when work arrives faster than it can immediately be processed.|As utilization approaches capacity, waiting time can grow sharply. Larger queues delay rejection but do not add service capacity. Bound queues, measure wait time and apply backpressure or shedding when deadlines can no longer be met.|
TLS termination|TLS termination decrypts a connection at an intermediary such as a load balancer or proxy.|The next hop may be plaintext or a separately authenticated encrypted connection. Termination boundaries determine certificate ownership and where sensitive data becomes visible. Document both hops; external HTTPS alone does not prove backend encryption.|
Idempotency|An idempotent operation has the same intended effect when repeated as when performed once.|A server can use an idempotency key to recognize retries and return the original outcome. Keys need a defined scope, retention policy and conflict behavior. Transport reliability cannot by itself prevent duplicate business operations.|
